Chapter 10: "The Country of the Lion King"

Chapter 10, Part 32: "——Aaaaaaah"

"So then, it's not like she actually created an entire alternate dimension with Yin magic, is it?"

"If she could do that, Mother would be able to create worlds at will. Even for Mother, she'd never have enough mana for something like that."

"Ah, so it's a mana problem? You're saying if that were solved, she could do something like a creator god?"

"It wouldn't be surprising if she could... That's just how amazing Mother is."

Walking through the devastated interior of the castle, Beatrice spoke proudly of her terrifying mother's power.

Having refrained from digging into the past until now, thinking those might be old wounds, Beatrice's pride in her parent Donna continued endlessly, as if she had an inexhaustible supply of stories she wanted to share.

Part of it was surely because she was actually walking through Castle Kugelschreiber for the first time in four hundred years on this homecoming. —Even if what she saw was a scene where the castle's former glory had been mercilessly destroyed.

"But knowing you think about her like that, Beako, I'm kinda jealous."

"Honestly, Subaru and Mother compete in completely different categories. Mother is the absolute, immovable number one in the family rankings, and Subaru is the immovable number one in Betty's rankings."

"Wait, what's that? That makes me super happy. Does that mean I beat Puck?"

"You're tied for first with Nii-cha."

"Curse that fluffy furball... He stands in the way of becoming both Emilia-tan's number one and Beako's number one...!"

The image of the cat spirit stroking his whiskers with a "nyahaha" and a smug face floated up, and Subaru gnashed his teeth at the height of the enemy he had to overcome. He had to be careful, or he'd end up swooning over that fur too.

And while they were having that exchange—

"—It's here."

Beatrice stopped dead in her tracks, and "Here?" Subaru and the others tilted their heads.

After all, what she turned to face was just an ordinary corridor wall. Inside the castle, along the path they'd carefully navigated over collapsed footing, it was just one wall.

But Beatrice wagged her raised finger left and right with a "tch tch tch" and said,

"The owner of this castle is a 'Witch.' It would be utterly ridiculous for records left behind by that 'Witch' to just be sitting out in a normal room. So—"

Saying that, Beatrice gently touched the wall with the finger she'd been wagging.

Nothing happened. With her finger still against the wall, Beatrice extended her other hand and called softly, "Subaru, Subaru." He hurriedly grasped it.

Mana flowed through Subaru into Beatrice, and light was born from the tip of her finger against the wall. It formed a single door of light on the blank wall—

"I've seen this once before at the castle entrance."

"There's no reason to make a different one when the best version is already made. Now, feast your eyes. —This is the 'Witch' Echidna's private study!"

Beyond the wall that had become passable, what greeted Subaru and the others as they finally stepped in was air that felt even heavier than when they'd first entered the castle, the scent of wood that had accumulated a long silence, and a room whose contents were all too fitting for a "Witch."

"This is... quite something, indeed."

"Kuhahaha, His Grace the Duke even chooses his words gracefully and modestly! This kind of thing, you should just come right out and say it—creepy! Bad taste! Gloomy! That's what would do the person a favor! Oops, but the person herself is already dead, huh—?"

"B-Black Dog! How can you say such things! I'm so sorry, Miss Librarian!"

Black Dog, who had looked around the room and spun about, earned a hurried bow of apology from White Sheep. But Beatrice graciously extended a palm, saying "It matters not,"

"Being feared and dreaded is also necessary for a 'Witch'... Rather, this reaction is exactly what Mother expected. There's no need to apologize."

"Man, your feelings toward your old lady are seriously twisted..."

"Ehh, Long Tongue-kun is the one saying that..."

Ferris voiced a simple question to Rachins, who had his lips twisted.

Indeed, Rachins had deliberately left a wealthy household to run with a gang of thugs. Even if not his parents, there was a strong suspicion of family troubles.

In any case—

"Black Dog's way of putting it is a bit much, but there's no denying this is something new."

Even Russell was affirming Black Dog's expression, and such was the scene unfolding.

The study was a large space with a high ceiling and numerous black pillars supporting it, one that could easily be called a great hall. Several pedestal-like workbenches were lined up in the room, but what drew the eye were the countless crystals of various sizes and colors on top of them—

"...This really feels like a 'Witch's' laboratory."

Peering closely at a nearby crystal, Subaru murmured quietly.

In the palm-sized crystal before him, a butterfly with wings spread was sealed. Next to it, a plant preserved not just with petals but even the roots extending into soil, then a lizard-like small animal curled up.

All of them were specimens preserved so perfectly in color and form that they looked like they might start moving at any moment.

"Not just small ones—there are demon beasts here too. Take a look. That one is a large-scale... what was it called?"

"This is astonishing. It's a Guiltylowe. To think a demon beast could be preserved in this form."

Among the group examining the crystal specimens, Kursch and Russell, who had gone toward the back, voiced their admiration. Before them was a crystal as large as a dragon carriage, with a large demon beast trapped inside.

Beyond that, there were fish, birds, demon beasts seen for the first time, and the variety of specimens was diverse.

"For what purpose would she collect so many living things... Hyah!"

"Oi oi, you idiot! Don't touch things without permission! Reckless and careless is one thing, but this is the height of it!"

"I-I'm sorry! But, um, look at this!"

Having let out a small scream and been scolded by Black Dog, White Sheep pointed at a crystal she had apparently touched. Inside it was a crimson deer with bright red fur, but it wasn't merely trapped. —Light was born inside the crystal, and the deer's body became transparent, revealing its internal organs, bones, and even countless lines of light running through it.

"That—don't tell me, you can see not just the inside of the body, but the gates too?"

Ferris murmured with a voice that didn't hide his shudder at what he saw. He widened his yellow eyes, touched a crystal near him, and confirmed the same phenomenon.

"How is it, Ferris? What can you tell?"

"...The ones inside aren't alive. But it's amazing. When something dies, the Od slowly disperses, but this—the shape of the Od from when it was alive has been copied exactly as it was."

"Copying the Od... In other words, the shape of the soul has been recorded?"

"Yes. ...At least, I think it's something close to that."

Perhaps the shock was considerable, as Ferris didn't even seem to notice that Kursch was speaking to him, trembling at this glimpse of the "Witch's" research.

Subaru too, at that explanation, looked toward Beatrice and said,

"Looks like the 'Witch's study' sign isn't false advertising. Did Beako ever hear what she was doing in here?"

"...Mother didn't like showing her research to others. She was careful about what knowledge she passed on, too. There must have been unpleasant memories."

"Unpleasant memories... Well, when you're smart or strong, all sorts of things happen."

Thinking about it that way, there was a gap in thinking between the young Donna who prided herself on being asked for advice, and the parent Donna Beatrice described. Surely, young Donna had unpleasant experiences, and the result of her values changing was parent Donna—he could feel that flow of time, but—

"These experimental tools are interesting, but ideally I'd like to find materials we can read and interpret. Librarian, do you have any idea where?"

"There's a small room in the back that I was told not to enter."

"A small room, you mean this?"

To Russell, who urged that just looking at experimental equipment wouldn't get them anywhere, Beatrice pointed to a corner of the study. Subaru put his hand on the door there and opened it wide. A musty scent of old books overflowed, and he involuntarily waved a hand in front of his face.

And then—

"Whoa, this is... where do we even start? This is brutal."

From behind Subaru who had murmured that, the others who peered inside the same way also groaned.

The room Beatrice called small might indeed be small compared to the study that was as large as a great hall, but it was still about the size of Roswaal Manor's dining hall.

In a room of that size, books, bundles of paper, and scrolls were crammed in densely and haphazardly.

"I told you, didn't I. —Mother wasn't very good at tidying up."

"I wish there were limits to that. For the sake of future generations."

Picking up two books that had fallen right by the door at his feet, Subaru felt despair at the fact that neither had a title and apparently had no relation or continuity to each other.

"The Forbidden Library, and now the Pleiades Watchtower..."

Natsuki Subaru, who generally had no good memories when it came to other people's libraries, found this to be yet another library that seemed destined for the same category of memories—truly a troublesome library.

△▼△▼△▼△

—In the end, organizing the "Witch's" library took several hours.

The work, tackled by all eight of them, involved first righting fallen bookshelves, gathering scattered books, bundling loose paper stacks, carefully recovering torn scrolls—a series of detailed, major tasks.

And after all that, they had only finally reached the starting line of organization, which just went to show that cleaning up someone else's library was not something to be undertaken. The one silver lining was that, at the very least, there was no chance of Subaru and the others missing anything they needed.

"—This one, and this one. And this material too, into the box. We'll take them back and examine them."

Saying that, Russell gazed at the collected items that had been categorized as much as possible, and using his "Appraiser's Blessing" to select those of value, decided which materials to take out.

The selected materials had no common format, and the writing and diagrams recorded in them were not consistent in any way either.

"This last one is just like an endless flower observation diary... Is it worth anything?"

"I don't know. But if I don't start from believing it might be, I can't live with these eyes. Indeed, there was meaning in not writing you all off, wasn't there?"

"Hah, nice thing to say. You were about to throw them away until I said something."

"Yes, the fact that I listened to your words is precisely because you have value."

"This guy, he seriously pisses me off...!"

The one who'd made the sarcastic remark ended up feeling bad, and Rachins ground his teeth.

Despite often being dragged into Russell's important conversations, the more time passed, the more these two seemed to be increasing friction rather than growing closer.

However—

"Aaah, ugh! Done, cleaned up! What a tiring, exhausting day! I've decided! From now on, anyone who looks like they'd read a lot of books, I'll kill them before they can buy a lot of books!"

"It's not necessarily the case that one has the opportunity to search the library of everyone who owns many books."

"There's no point saying that, Lady Verdant. It's just Black Dog-kun's usual idle talk."

"I see, that's right. I said something unnecessary. You may continue."

"Do it or don't! I'm tired and exhausted!"

Black Dog threw his body onto a workbench and howled like a big child. His gestures were childlike, but his frame was large so it wasn't cute, yet Subaru sympathized with his complaint of fatigue.

"The one-night time limit is still in effect, and just because this place is an alternate dimension, there's no perk like time flowing slower than outside, right?"

"As far as Betty knows, no. If there were such a perk, this castle would have remained in better condition... Though with 'Melancholy' involved, even that's doubtful."

The effects of aging from being abandoned were certainly a factor, but most of the castle's devastation came from the power of the "Melancholy Demon" who had rampaged here. Probably, it was a clash between "Witch" and "Demon" beyond what could be called a mere parent-child quarrel.

"Let's take our findings back and confirm them. Librarian, are there materials anywhere besides this room?"

"There shouldn't be. Other than this room, for the same reason I didn't put those kinds of books in the 'Forbidden Library,' there shouldn't be any elsewhere."

Answering Russell while glancing toward Subaru, Beatrice received a nod in return.

If the theory that she didn't want Beatrice to see soul-related books was adopted, then the library she was forbidden from entering must surely be the hiding place for those forbidden books.

If that was certain, then all that remained was to bring the harvest back—

"—Hey, Librarian-chan, is that all?"

"Ferris?"

At Ferris's suddenly tilted-head question, Subaru furrowed his brow.

He understood Ferris's desperate feelings, but Beatrice's cooperative attitude had been evident all along. Subaru couldn't overlook her being doubted like that.

But before Subaru could say anything, Ferris bent forward, met Beatrice's gaze, and said,

"You haven't been here in ages, right. And even while Silver Fox was comparing materials just now, you kept glancing outside the room."

"Eh. W-was that true, Beako?"

"Mm... I-it's nothing at all. Both Subaru and Ferris are overthinking things."

Beatrice waved her hands with a flustered face at Subaru and Ferris who peered into her face. A cute resistance, but—

"—The wind of lies is blowing."

Kursch, narrowing the eye not covered by her eyepatch, easily exposed it with her lie-detecting ability.

At that pointed observation, Beatrice tried to endure with "Gnnnn" for a while, but eventually gave up with a sigh,

"It's nothing, really. I just remembered. When we fled the castle from 'Melancholy,' there was no time to pack properly. Things I couldn't take then might still be in this castle, in Betty's room... that's all."

"I see. Are they important?"

"They wouldn't be a problem to lose. ...But they were a present from Mother."

Beatrice cast her eyes down and finished with those words, looking pitiful.

While feeling a pang in his chest at Beatrice's state, Subaru also tasted frustration. At his own inadequacy in letting Ferris be the first to notice Beatrice's lingering regret.

But moping about it would be foolish.

"Russell-san, can we go pick up a four-hundred-year-old forgotten item? I know full well it's unrelated to the operation..."

"If you know, then please exercise restraint. Have you forgotten our mission?"

"I can't argue with that..."

"But we've made it this far thanks to Librarian-chan, right? And yet, not even a single reward for Librarian-chan—that's not right, is it?"

It was Ferris once again who came to Subaru's aid when he was pushed back by the correct argument. To him voicing an opinion favoring Beatrice, Russell directed a slightly stern look,

"—. Don't tell me this is retaliation, Blue Snake."

"Now that's the last thing. But don't forget, okay? Gray Star-kun and Librarian-chan aren't your subordinates. And yet, even though they have people they want to see, they've been accompanying you for two months. You could at least show a little, couldn't you? Good faith."

"You..."

His sharp tongue was as harsh as ever, but when its target was directed at someone else rather than himself, it felt this reassuring—Subaru experienced both admiration and confusion.

He didn't understand why Ferris was being so considerate toward them. Of course, no matter how foul his mouth was, Subaru trusted in the righteousness of his heart that had mastered healing magic, but.

"Don't look at me like that. If there are forgotten things that can be recovered, it's better to recover them, right?"

"That's... true. Sorry. You really are a good guy."

"—. That's the thing about you, Subaru-kun. I hate it."

Ferris turned his face away with a "hmph," and Subaru scratched his cheek with a finger, smiling wryly. Meanwhile, at Ferris's request, Russell had been touching his chin beard with a difficult expression for a while, but—

"Um, Silver Fox, I'd like to ask too. We don't know when we'll be able to come back here again. That would be too sad for Librarian-san."

"White Sheep, even you."

"Gray Star-san and the others are in a different situation from me and Black Dog. Even you can't force them. You should already understand that, right?"

Tightening her cheeks, even White Sheep provided supporting fire for Subaru and the others.

Even for the compassionate her, voicing an opinion to her superior Russell must have taken considerable courage. He didn't think he'd been impressed by her stance, but—

"—Don't stay long. Recover what you need and rejoin us promptly."

After a moment of closed eyes, Russell's concession was successfully obtained.

△▼△▼△▼△

"I'll prioritize packing the materials. Gather at the entrance."

"I'm telling you, I'm not coming along either. Like I'd babysit some kid's trip down memory lane."

Leaving behind Russell, who prioritized the work, and Rachins, who was unsociable, the other members departed Castle Kugelschreiber heading for Beatrice's private room.

Unexpectedly, many had said they'd come along with Subaru and the others, and Rachins, left alone with Russell, ended up seeing them off with a look that said this was a miscalculation.

"But seriously, you came along too, huh."

"Kuhahaha, it's just that this is better than helping carry luggage—that's all. Besides, if the clumsy fool White Sheep tripped and fell into a hole, that'd be a disaster, right?"

"I-I'm not so clumsy that you need to worry about that... Kya!"

"See, laugh and scold! Right after I say it, this is what happens!"

Laughing loudly, Black Dog grabbed White Sheep's collar as she nearly fell and pulled her upright.

He couldn't help but smile wryly at White Sheep who fulfilled the expected pattern so perfectly, but Black Dog, despite his words, was quite overprotective of her.

Usually White Sheep felt more like the guardian, but now it seemed reversed.

"You two have been together since before joining the 'Six Tongues'... and you were 'Beloved Children,' right?"

"...I am, yes. Black Dog too, probably."

"Probably? Is that part vague?"

At White Sheep's uncertain answer, Subaru furrowed his brow in puzzlement. He'd assumed White Sheep and Black Dog were in the "Six Tongues" under the same circumstances.

"Oops, truth or lie, lie or truth! This woman with the hypocrite face is actually a wicked liar who looooves deceiving you all—!"

"The wind of lies is blowing. Your words are ill, White Sheep's words are good."

"Kah—! Boring, dull, and a mood killer!"

The mischief Black Dog interjected was cut down by Kursch's blessing. But even that seemed like a follow-up to divert attention from White Sheep's statement.

Indeed, Kursch was also guaranteeing that White Sheep's words were true.

"Actually, I don't really understand my relationship with Black Dog either. That we're close... that much should be certain, but I can't figure out the specifics."

"By the way, I haven't got a clue either! The feeling of 'when I noticed, I was there'—that's me, and 'I just happened to be there too'—that's her."

"What's that, that makes no sense at all."

"Yes, it makes no sense at all. —That's probably, my reason."

Clenching her white fist, White Sheep said that with a somewhat resolute expression.

Having brought it up himself, Subaru wondered if he should have heard this, and felt a little daunted. Once again, he'd rudely trampled into someone's heart.

"Even if 'trampling into people's hearts with muddy boots' is our family motto, there's a etiquette to trampling too, so when it happens without that intent, it's alarming."

"What are you mumbling about. Come here, don't get lost."

Beatrice pulled his pondering arm, and he followed her as she led the group.

At first, Beatrice had been somewhat apologetic about having them accompany her forgotten-item search, but as she walked through her childhood home that she knew like the back of her hand, her stiffness gradually faded.

Now her eyes shone with anticipation for the reunion with the forgotten item she'd soon recover.

"By the way, what was Echidna's—Mother's present?"

"Mm, that's something to look forward to seeing in person. Wanting to know everything so impatiently isn't just Subaru—it's a bad habit of all humans."

"Well, compared to Beako who has a four-hundred-year waiting record, anyone would."

Beatrice proudly puffed out her chest, and Subaru smiled wryly at her small strides leading the way. Watching her swinging drill twin-tails, there was nothing but endearment in her bouncing steps.

Perhaps everyone else felt the same, as no one tried to dampen Beatrice's anticipation for the present across four hundred years.

"And yet, it keeps getting in the way..."

Subaru grumbled thus—a complaint directed at the "Melancholy Demon" whose destructive influence remained thick throughout the castle no matter where they went.

Every time they found where Melancholy's rampage had collapsed ceilings, broken pillars, and cracked floors, they had to detour around impassable paths, taking the long way toward Beatrice's room.

Now again, cutting through a half-collapsed room, shortcutting through a crushed corridor—ahead of that, they encountered a place where a thick pillar lay horizontally, blocking the corridor.

"Whoa, another detour?"

"—. No, look. It seems we can get through beside the pillar."

The pillar was large enough that an adult's arms couldn't reach around it, but as Beatrice pointed out, there was a gap between it and the right wall that one person at a time could squeeze through.

"That said, I'm nervous not being able to see the other side. How about I go first? Beako waits her turn."

"Betty is compact and cute, so she could pass through without danger."

"I'm pretty flexible too, you know. I do the pigeon pose every night."

With yoga spirituality pushed forward, Beatrice yielded the first-penguin role to Subaru.

Subaru lowered his head, twisted his body into the gap beside the pillar, and slipped through to the other side. Fortunately, the snagging he'd feared and a pitfall trap right after didn't materialize, and the corridor seemed to continue ahead—it wasn't a dead end either.

"Okay. It's passable. Be careful not to catch your hair or dress."

Brushing the knees of his tracksuit where they'd touched the floor, Subaru called out to Beatrice on the other side of the pillar. He crouched down, waiting for Beatrice to come through the gap—

"—Beako?"

Beatrice had a small body. She should have been able to slip through the gap more easily than Subaru, yet her figure never appeared on this side no matter how long he waited. Peering impatiently through the gap beside the pillar, Beatrice wasn't there—no, that wasn't it.

"—! Hey, you're kidding, right?"

Doubting his own eyes, Subaru lowered his head again and passed through the gap, this time in the opposite direction. Frustrated, scraping his head against the pillar, he emerged into the corridor on the other side and raised his face.

And—

"—No one, is here?"

Standing up in the corridor where he'd been walking with everyone just moments ago, Subaru looked around in bewilderment. Beatrice should have been there. Kursch, Ferris, White Sheep, Black Dog—none of them were there.

Not a single member who should have come together was present. They had vanished without a trace.

"That's, impossible... Beatrice! Beako! Answer me! Beako—!!"

Raising his voice loudly, Subaru called Beatrice's name at the top of his lungs. His voice echoed emptily through the old castle, but the answer he sought didn't return.

The surging impatience instantly soaked Subaru's back with cold sweat.

"Kursch-san! Ferris!! White Sheep-chan! Black Dog! Where are you! Beako! Someone! Anyone! Answer me!!"

Unable to stay still, Subaru called out his companions' names one after another. But no one answered. Not just Beatrice—Kursch and the other four gave no reply either. —That was an abnormality too obvious to miss.

"What in the world... Don't tell me, Echidna!?"

This was the "Greed Witch's" castle, and from the moment they'd discovered the hidden materials room if not a forbidden library, he'd had a bad premonition that some kind of mechanism might be at work.

However, Beatrice hadn't mentioned anything about it, and until now—whether "Trials" or "Tests"—there had always been a trigger for something to happen.

So his vigilance had gradually faded—

"Idiot, me. No, I'm an idiot... Letting my guard down against Echidna!"

Biting his lip hard, he admonished himself with the pain. There was no time to waste on lengthy self-reproach. He had to find the vanished Beatrice and the others immediately. Or else rejoin Russell and the others.

And then, conquer the mechanism of Castle Kugelschreiber—

"—Huh?"

Just as he thought that.

It happened when he took the first step to retrace the corridor he'd come through. —Without any warning, the footing he stepped on gave way without resistance, and his body tilted.

By the time he thought "Damn," it was too late. —The next moment, his body was thrown into the air.

"—!!"

The floor gave out, and he was flung outside the corridor. The instant he realized it, Subaru drew the Guilty Whip from behind his waist and desperately lashed it at the corridor he'd slipped through. —Luckily, the tip caught on a crack or something that had formed in the corridor.

"Guh!" A fierce impact ran through his right shoulder that held the whip, and Subaru's body caught.

"W-w-w-what..."

He had no idea what had happened.

Without sign, without omen, without forewarning, without any trigger, the floor fell out. Thrown outside the corridor just like that, Subaru looked around and shuddered.

—There was nothing. It was an empty void, as if the creation of heaven and earth had never occurred.

"——"

If you fall, you can absolutely never come back—that's what Beatrice had said at the beginning. The meaning of that was crystal clear to Subaru, who was now half-falling.

Indeed, it was impossible. Fall here and you can't return. There's no way back. You won't be found.

So—

"I have to somehow get back up..."

It was just a lifeline caught in a crack in the floor, unreliable.

To keep it from coming loose, he wrapped the whip around his wrist, and little by little—yet urgently—carefully reeled his body up, up, up—

"Eh?"

The snap of impact was sudden.

If put to sound, it was a snap. But he heard no sound. There was no room for that.

"—Ah."

Mercilessly, the lifeline supporting Subaru's body lost its taut tension and, as if mocking his desperate struggle for life, flung his body into the void.

"A, aaaaaaah—!!"

Screaming, Subaru's body spun round and round vertically, backward, losing all sense of up and down, as he rapidly receded from the corridor—no, from Castle Kugelschreiber.

Receding, receding, receding, falling. Into a literally pitch-black abyss.

"I, saw..."

His whole body battered by violent wind, tossed by tremendous impact, a voice escaped him.

He saw it. With these eyes, he saw it. The moment the whip came loose and he was thrown off—he saw it. —The snap, and the tip of the whip that came off the corridor exposed a brand-new, sharply cut edge.

Someone had cut Subaru's whip, severing his lifeline.

Severed, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling, fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall fall——

"——"

It didn't come.

Gritting his teeth hard, Subaru braced his whole body for the impact of the ground that should come.

He tried to find a way, but immediately realized there was nothing he could do. Beatrice wasn't there. There was nowhere for the whip to reach. He was already tens, hundreds of meters away from the castle.

No way back, no means of survival. So all that remained was to prepare for the "death" that should come, and at the very least, to share this predicament with everyone—he resolved himself desperately.

Come. Come anytime. Come. Come now. Come immediately. Come now. Come. Quickly, now, everyone's in danger. Come right now. Come. Come. Come. Come, I said. Come!

"——"

—It didn't come.

"——"

He realized. After steeling himself, he realized.

He should have understood it himself earlier. This was a world where the creation of heaven and earth had never occurred. No matter how far he fell, there was no earth to collide with and shatter Subaru's body into pieces.

Even if he kept falling like this, endlessly, endlessly, he would never reach anywhere.

"——"

He desperately tried to deny the terrifying possibility that surfaced in his mind.

But the greatest ally of denial—the collision with the ground—didn't come. Still, wind and fall whipped Subaru's body around, and his speed continued to increase.

Eventually, his vision blacked out.

The rotation of his body disrupted his circulation, blood rushing excessively to his head and the extremities of his limbs. Fierce pain began running through his whole body, and just like that, Subaru's consciousness—

△▼△▼△▼△

"——"

It doesn't end. This fall has no end.

How much time he spent accepting that fact, Subaru didn't know.

He just kept falling, his body never stopping its rotation, and from the blood flow disruption that caused, he lost consciousness countless times, regained it without trigger, and was forced to realize the nightmare situation was reality.

His stomach cried of hunger, his soiled lower body had no sensation, his eyes were long past useless.

Even so, Natsuki Subaru was alive. He had gone and stayed alive.

"—Aaaaaaah."

Groaning lowly like that, Subaru cursed his own slowness to judge, his stupidity.

His body, continuing to fall and be tossed by wind, had lost its freedom, and his limbs no longer moved as he wished. While his hands still worked, he could have strangled himself.

But he couldn't anymore. There was no strength anywhere. Likewise, he didn't even have the strength left to bite his tongue, and every means to lead himself to death had been lost.

"—Aaaaaaah."

Even now, as Subaru falls meaninglessly, Beatrice and the others are in danger.

In the castle he can no longer return to, Beatrice and the others might still be exposed to threats right now. —And yet, he can't go back for them. He can't even die.

"—Aaaaaaah."

No matter how much Subaru wishes for the end, his lungs crave air, his heart sends blood through his body with disordered flow, and the minimum vital functions continue.

Time in which he can do nothing, can only not die, is stretched out endlessly, futilely, meaninglessly.

"—Aaaaaaah."

His throat was parched.

From screaming, from shedding tears, from wetting himself, from draining all the moisture in his body, he was bone-dry.

His body was freezing cold.

Blood flow disrupted, tossed by wind for what felt like an eternity, having done not a single activity that generates heat.

His soul was screaming.

It sought Beatrice, everyone in the castle, Emilia, his separated companions.

"—Aaaaaaah."

Voice—not a scream, not a shout, but a groaning sound—he kept emitting.

From the very depths, depths of depths of depths of his body, as if squeezing it out, he kept emitting voice.

To hurry, to squeeze it out even a second sooner, to die.

There was nothing else he could do. —No, there was. There was more than just groaning. To decide. To firmly swear in his heart, to decide, and to absolutely carry it out.

He would not let go. He would absolutely not let go. He would not let go of Beatrice.

"—Aaaaaaah."

No matter what happened, he would absolutely not let go.

"—Aaaaaaah."

